first  appeared  in (primary author) (Byteswapped file system and
UFS2 support) A big bug‐finding kudos goes to John Kohl for find‐
ing a significant rotational layout bug.  Can fail  to  shrink  a
file  system when there actually is enough space, because it does
not distinguish between a block allocated as a block and a  block
fully  occupied  by two or more frags.  This is unlikely to occur
in practice; except for pathological cases, it  can  happen  only
when  the  new  size  is extremely close to the minimum possible.
Has no intelligence whatever when it comes to  allocating  blocks
to  copy  data  into  when shrinking.  Does not currently support
shrinking FFSv2 file systems.
